                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Adult room, clean output priority</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Warm mist</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Boiling kills most microbes in the water</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            If you are still undecided, cool mist is the choice that is hardest to regret. It is cheaper to run, safer around everyone, and with a sensible cleaning routine just as hygienic in practice. Our <a href="/best-humidifier/">best humidifier guide</a> lists both types, and the comparison of <a href="/evaporative-vs-ultrasonic-humidifier/">evaporative and ultrasonic</a> designs will help you pick the right cool mist unit.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying Warm Mist to Heat the Room</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A warm mist humidifier draws a few hundred watts and puts most of that into making vapor. It will not noticeably warm a bedroom. If the room is cold, fix the heating and buy whichever humidifier suits your safety and cost needs rather than paying extra for warmth you will not feel.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Putting Warm Mist Within a Child's Reach</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Cool-touch housings and tip-over shut-offs reduce the risk but do not remove the hot water in the base. A toddler pulling the cord or grabbing the unit can be scalded in seconds. In any room a child sleeps or plays in, use cool mist and keep cords tucked away.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Filling an Ultrasonic Unit With Hard Tap Water</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Ultrasonic humidifiers put dissolved minerals into the air, where they settle as fine white dust on furniture and electronics. If your water is hard, use distilled or demineralized water, add the manufacturer's demineralization cartridge, or choose an evaporative or warm mist model instead.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping the Descale on Warm Mist</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Boiling leaves minerals behind as hard scale on the heating element and chamber. Left alone it slows heating, raises energy use and can shorten the unit's life. Descale with white vinegar or citric acid as often as the manual directs, and more often with hard water.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Adding Essential Oils to the Tank</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Unless a humidifier has a dedicated aroma pad or tray, oils and additives can damage ultrasonic transducers and plastic tanks and may void the warranty. Dispersed oils can also irritate airways and are unsafe around some pets, especially cats. Keep the tank to water only.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is warm mist or cool mist better for a baby's room?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Cool mist. Common pediatric guidance, including advice from the American Academy of Pediatrics and Mayo Clinic, recommends cool mist humidifiers for children because warm mist units contain near-boiling water that can scald if the unit is pulled over or touched. Both types add the same moisture to the air, so there is no comfort reason to accept the burn risk in a nursery.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does a warm mist humidifier warm up the room?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not noticeably. A typical warm mist unit draws roughly 200 to 400 watts, and much of that energy goes into vaporizing water rather than heating air. A portable space heater on high typically uses 1,500 watts. Properly humidified air does feel a little warmer on your skin, but that is true whichever type of humidifier raised the humidity.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Which is better for colds and congestion?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Both add the same amount of moisture, and general medical guidance notes that humidified air can make breathing feel easier when you are congested. Neither treats an illness. Warm mist offers no meaningful extra benefit and adds a burn risk, so cool mist is the usual recommendation, especially in homes with children. See a doctor for symptoms that persist or worsen.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do cool mist humidifiers spread bacteria?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                They can if the tank is not cleaned. Ultrasonic units disperse whatever is in the water, including microbes that grow in a neglected tank. Empty and dry the tank daily, clean it as the manual directs, usually weekly, and use fresh water each time. With that routine, a cool mist humidifier is hygienic in normal household use.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much more does warm mist cost to run?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Using typical ratings, a 300-watt warm mist unit running eight hours a night for a month uses about 72 kilowatt-hours, roughly $12 at 17 cents per kilowatt-hour. A 30-watt ultrasonic unit uses about 7.2 kilowatt-hours, around $1.22. Check your model's wattage and your own electricity rate, because both vary widely.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the white dust around my humidifier?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It is minerals from hard tap water, dispersed by an ultrasonic humidifier and settled on nearby surfaces. It is a nuisance and the EPA advises low-mineral water in humidifiers. Switch to distilled or demineralized water, add a demineralization cartridge, or use an evaporative or warm mist model, both of which leave minerals behind in the unit.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I use a hybrid humidifier with both modes?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. Hybrid units usually pair an ultrasonic transducer with a heating element that warms the water before it is misted. Many warm the water rather than boiling it, so do not assume the warm setting sterilizes the water unless the manufacturer says so. Use the cool setting in any room children or pets can reach.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

